humans have a poor track record with  understanding mental illness in ancient  times depression and anxiety were seen  as demonic possessions or supernatural  ailments people treated them with  religious rites and mystic rituals for  every civilization that rose and fell  there were just as many  misunderstandings and so-called cures  for people who experience the world  differently than their friends or peers  by the 18th century many had come to  vilify people with mental illnesses and  finding them to asylums deeming them a  menace to what we labeled normal society  fortunately in 2017 we have a more  compassionate albeit still developing  approach to mental illness we've  accepted that it's a pervasive obstacle  for people of all ages many of  experienced depression or anxiety  schizophrenia or bipolar disorder and  many have dedicated their careers to  studying understanding and treating  these problems but when it comes to art  and entertainment these problems are  still by and large taboo subjects  literature has tackled them sometimes  portraying stories from the perspective  of people suffering from depression or  post-traumatic stress disorder  mainstream movies have used mental  illness to provide shock value grotesque  villains or even comic relief the ones  we remember for their empathy and  authenticity are few and far between  so it caught me off guard to find such  empathy for mental illness in a  videogame of all places as someone who  has seen depression anxiety PTSD and  bipolar disorder up close it came as a  shock to find such an authentic take on  psychosis it's such a young medium one  originally built on fun competition and  escapism no less  not only is hell-blade Sen who has  sacrificed a great game in its own right  but one of the most honest and  empathetic portrayals of a mentally ill  character I've seen in entertainment  period  [Music]  hell-blade opens on senua a Celtic  Warrior on something like a Vision Quest  there are voices all around her it's not  too late to get into the boat at several  points she addresses the camera without  saying much at all hell-blade makes it  clear Senna is suffering this becomes  all the more apparent when we realize  that our surroundings aren't necessarily  real or at least not to anyone but senua  that is much of what we're seeing is  playing out in her mind she has  psychosis a symptom of mental illness  that can manifest in several different  ways but mainly hallucinations delusions  and a general detachment from the  objective world developer Ninja Theory  takes the traditional hero's journey  story structure and places it in a  mental health CAPTCHA paisa Noah's  psychosis it echos her chaotic tormented  mind she calls her symptoms of the  darkness and it permeates every aspect  of her environment if you suffer from  depression or anxiety then you know how  difficult they are to grasp their  impetuous there are elusive things  hell-blade illustrates Senna was coping  methods in the way she gives form to her  psychosis she creates physical  manifestations that are easier to fight  than the insidious emotions that mental  illnesses breathe we can't always battle  what we can't see  [Music]  seonhwa's personal struggles also  parallel those of North's mythology as  an off-screen character relays the  legends of Odin's Thor Loki and frere  these tales foreshadow the coming of  Ragnarok essentially it's the Norse  apocalypse when gods battle hellish  monsters and natural disasters resulting  in the end of one world and the  beginning of the next and the farther  into hell sena descends the closer she  gets to Ragnarok and the further the  story progresses the more we come to  realize just how much sin OA psychosis  has shattered her life this was a time  long before any cursory understanding of  mental illness existed her fellow tribes  people thought she was cursed someone  close to her treated her with cruelty  isolating her and severing her  connections with the outside world  what's more hell-blade explores the  pressure mental illness can put on a  relationship how it can cause rifts  between two people who care about each  other very much how it can give rise to  guilt when there's nothing they can do  to fight through one person's mental  anguish in one of the more clever moves  in recent game design memory ninja  theory tells you upfront that if you die  enough times  seonhwa will succumb to a disease that  sets her back to the beginning of her  journey as players this means we lose  our save file now at the publishing of  this video it's actually unclear how  this permadeath works or if it actually  works at all  some claim it's intentionally foggy on  ninja theories part while others say  it's an outright lie I can't tell you  one thing for certain though each time I  came close to death I began to sweat  each time I died I grew even more  anxious whether a permadeath system is  actually in place is beside the point  Ninja Theory effectively placed me in  the shoes of someone with irrational  dread and an omnipresent fear psychosis  can bring about both of these hell-blade  found a way to put me in some semblance  of that position but for all of the  oppressive aspects of Hell blaze world  and for all of the trials Santa WA  undergoes on her journey what's most  remarkable is how Ninja Theory handled  these heavy subjects throughout a seven  hour playthrough the studio never once  exploits cinema for shock value or  spectacle it uses her mental illness to  influence the gameplay in ways that make  sense for her character and also help us  under  and her emotions seonhwa's delusions  leader to see connections and signs in  the worlds that others might not she  creates connections in her surroundings  embracing her heightened imagination in  a way that aids her navigation through  her demonic hero's journey furthermore  although the voices in Santa's head are  often negative and sadistic they also  alert her to flanking enemies and help  her navigate the darkness depression  anxiety and bipolar disorder can often  warp your perception of time and here  too he'll bleed connects enemas  perspective with the way she navigates  her world  seonhwa may see her hallucinations and  capricious outlook as curses but they  can also help her sort through her grief  and loss they can help her cope with  trauma when no one else can understand  what she's feeling now we can't define  people by their mental illness they  contain multitudes after all and Ninja  Theory knows this Senna is a warrior a  lover and an ambitious person she is a  human being who happens to be battling a  painful combination of guilt confusion  and despair and it's all weakening her  mind this is her personal journey to not  only confront but also to conquer her  illness to use her humanity and  imagination to stare her grief in the  face and to hopefully drive a sword  through its heart  but there's nothing to go back to  naturalistic hell-blade is a remarkable  depiction of mental health problems not  just for how it conveys the symptoms and  emotions of people who suffer from them  but for how it empathizes with them as  well make a mistake this took guts on  the part of ninja theory it took courage  to play psychosis at hell-blade center  and even more to do so with authenticity  mental illness may be the center of this  game but empathy is its fuel this is the  story of Ragnarok but it's not one of  mythical proportions and godly battles  this is cinemas personal Ragnarok one  that signifies the end of one world and  the birth of another it's a game set in  a time with no understanding of mental  illness yet as a modern story it blazes  a trail  [Music]  [Music]
 

